<t tx="ekr.20261116100828.1065">@language md

See #4993.

### Overview

This PR adds only the the code necessary to rewrite c_ec.promoteToAtOthers using the following new undo pattern:
```python
with c.undoer as u:
    u.set_command_name(command_name)
    # Calls to new undoer helpers as well as any other Leonine code.
    # See  c_ec.promoteToAtOthers for a full example.
```

### Tasks

- [x] Add the UndoBead class.
- [x] Add `u.__enter__`, and `u.__exit__` methods to support the "with" pattern shown above.
- [x] Add all methods in the u.New helpers sub-outline.
- [x] Remove dummy declarations used only by pylint.
- [x] Rewrite c_ec.promoteToAtOthers using new Undoer methods.
- [x] Ensure that the new undoers work after changing c.p.

### Extras

- [x] Merge PR #4995 into this PR so that ci tests pass.

### Discussion

Compared with the legacy undo methods, the new code is a spectacular collapse in complexity.

The UndoBead class:

- Represents *any sequence* of undoable actions. There is no need for explicit grouping.
- The UndoBead class organizes undoable operations into two groups:
   Undo/redo **functions** to be called *before* calling c.redraw.
   Undo/redo **finishers** to be called *after* calling c.redraw.
- UndoBead.undo and UndoBead.redraw are amazingly simple. See the code the the details!
 
The new undo helpers (in the node "u.New helpers") contain all undo/redo code in one place instead of four helpers as in the legacy undo code. Each helper:
- Pre-computes all data required for one kind of undo or redo operations.
- Defines functions or finishers that bind these pre-computed data.
  In effect, all functions or finishers are closures.
- Appends the functions or finishers to the appropriate lists in the UndoBead instance.
- Sets the "sticky" UndoBead.must_redraw flag if necessary. This flag is *never* cleared.
- Executes the desired action.</t>
